<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- ================================================================== -->
<!-- Reader guide for the drag-and-drop research journal.               -->
<!-- ================================================================== -->
<chapter xml:id="ch-dnd-quick-start-guide">
  <title>Quick Start Guide</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This chapter summarizes a proposed framework and data pipeline for the 
      analysis of a drag-and-drop interactive question. The organizational 
      structure of this document is as follows:
      <md>
        \text{item design}
        \rightarrow
        \text{state model}
        \rightarrow
        \text{event trajectory}
        \rightarrow
        \text{features}
        \rightarrow
        \text{models}.
      </md>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-reader-guide-fast-map">
    <title>Fast Map of the Journal</title>

    <p>
      The journal has ten main chapters. They divide naturally into five blocks.
    </p>

    <table xml:id="tab-reader-guide-blocks">
      <title>Conceptual blocks in the drag-and-drop journal.</title>
      <tabular valign="top" bottom="minor">
        <col width="20%" halign="left"/>
        <col width="30%" halign="left"/>
        <col width="45%" halign="left"/>
        <row bottom="medium">
          <cell><p>Block</p></cell>
          <cell><p>Chapters</p></cell>
          <cell><p>What the block contributes</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Motivation and task structure</p></cell>
          <cell><p><xref ref="ch-dnd-anatomy" text="title"/>; <xref ref="ch-item-design" text="title"/></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Explains what a cardsort is, what the logs record, and which parts of the task are fixed before students act.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Representation</p></cell>
          <cell><p><xref ref="ch-state-model" text="title"/>; <xref ref="ch-event-trajectory-model" text="title"/></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Defines board states, membership matrices, moves, checks, resets, and timestamped trajectories.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Feature construction</p></cell>
          <cell><p><xref ref="ch-baseline-features" text="title"/>; <xref ref="ch-group-features" text="title"/></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Turns states and trajectories into measurable summaries: correctness, distance, error types, movement, timing, grouping, and alternative comparison metrics.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Data pipeline and diagnostics</p></cell>
          <cell><p><xref ref="ch-data-processing" text="title"/>; <xref ref="ch-visualization-diagnostics" text="title"/></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Explains how raw exports become analysis-ready tables and how feature summaries can diagnose item behavior.</p></cell>
        </row>
        <row>
          <cell><p>Inference and interpretation</p></cell>
          <cell><p><xref ref="ch-statistical-modeling" text="title"/>; <xref ref="ch-validity-interpretation" text="title"/></p></cell>
          <cell><p>Asks which patterns are stable, predictive, or interpretable, and states the limits of what interaction logs can support.</p></cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-reader-guide-guided-tour">
    <title>Overview of the Chapters</title>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-reader-guide-ch1">
      <title>Chapter 1: What Is Logged, and Why It Matters</title>

      <p>
        <xref ref="ch-dnd-anatomy" text="title"/> is the conceptual entry point.
        It explains the contrast between ordinary autograded data and drag-and-drop
        process data. The reader should come away understanding that the log is
        not only an outcome record. It contains an ordered trace of moves, checks,
        resets, feedback, and timestamps.
      </p>

      <p>
        The running example is deliberately small. Its purpose is not to be an
        interesting assessment item, but to create all of the structural cases the
        framework needs: cards that belong in slots, a card that should remain in
        the tray, and a slot that should remain empty.
      </p>

      <p>
        While reading, ask whether the motivation would be clear to someone who
        has never seen Runestone, PreTeXt, or DataShop. The chapter should make the
        reader believe that ordinary correctness data are missing something
        methodologically important.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-reader-guide-ch2">
      <title>Chapter 2: Separate the Task From the Student</title>

      <p>
        <xref ref="ch-item-design" text="title"/> defines the designed object:
        the cards, slots, tray, zones, key, decoys, and feedback policy. This is a
        crucial separation. Item design features describe the response space that
        the author created. They are not performance features, although they may
        explain differences in performance.
      </p>

      <p>
        The most important design idea is that decoys are explicit. A decoy card
        is a card whose home zone is the tray. A decoy slot is a slot that should
        remain empty. These two structures create observable boundary errors: a
        student may place a card that should remain unplaced, or fill a slot that
        should remain empty.
      </p>

      <p>
        The second important design idea is that feedback policy is part of the
        task. A student working with immediate card-level feedback is not solving
        the same task as a student receiving only an all-or-nothing verdict, even
        if the visible source item is otherwise identical.
      </p>

      <p>
        While reading, ask whether the item-design covariates are sufficient for
        later comparisons across items, semesters, or platform eras. In
        particular, check whether the feedback policy is described concretely
        enough to support modeling and interpretation.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-reader-guide-ch3">
      <title>Chapter 3: Represent One Board State</title>

      <p>
        <xref ref="ch-state-model" text="title"/> defines what a board snapshot
        is. A state is a function
        <m>s:
        \mathcal{P}\to\mathcal{Z}</m>, assigning every card to exactly one zone.
        The same state can be stored as a vector of zone indices or as a
        membership matrix <m>X</m> with one row per zone and one column per card.
      </p>

      <p>
        The vector representation is compact and implementation-friendly. The
        membership matrix representation is more algebraic: it makes agreement,
        distance, occupancy, and cohort averages easy to express. A reader should
        judge whether this algebraic payoff is worth the notation.
      </p>

      <p>
        The key state <m>X^\star</m> and the initial state <m>X_0</m> are the two
        reference states used throughout the rest of the framework. Most later
        features compare the current state either to the key, to the initial
        state, or to a previous state in the same trajectory.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-reader-guide-ch4">
      <title>Chapter 4: Turn States Into Trajectories</title>

      <p>
        <xref ref="ch-event-trajectory-model" text="title"/> adds time and action
        marks. A move changes the state by moving one card. A check leaves the
        state unchanged but marks it as submitted and attaches feedback. A reset
        jumps back to the initial state. A page event does not change the board,
        but may help delimit a session.
      </p>

      <p>
        The central object is the event trajectory
        <md>
          \Gamma=
          \bigl((a_0,t_0,X_0),(a_1,t_1,X_1),\dots,(a_n,t_n,X_n)\bigr).
        </md>
        This object is the bridge between interface logs and process features.
      </p>

      <p>
        The chapter also interprets moves as walks through a configuration graph.
        That graph view matters because it turns distance to the key into an
        operational quantity: how many single-card moves are needed to reach the
        key from the current state.
      </p>

      <p>
        While reading, ask whether the trajectory object is exactly rich enough:
        not so minimal that it loses checks, feedback, and time, but not so
        elaborate that it becomes difficult to reconstruct from real data.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-reader-guide-ch5">
      <title>Chapter 5: Define the Core Feature Vocabulary</title>

      <p>
        <xref ref="ch-baseline-features" text="title"/> is one of the central
        chapters. It defines the baseline features that make the data analyzable:
        checked-state correctness, distance to the key, error profiles, movement
        summaries, check behavior, timing, and cohort summaries.
      </p>

      <p>
        The key methodological point is that a feature is not an interpretation.
        A distance, a decoy-card leak, a takeback, or a long gap is a structural
        measurement derived from the item design, reconstructed states, and event
        log. Whether that measurement reflects guessing, confusion, strategic use
        of feedback, or learning is a later validity question.
      </p>

      <p>
        Read this chapter closely. Ask which features are essential for a first
        article and which are optional. A strong publishable version may need a
        smaller core vocabulary plus a clear argument that these features are
        robust, computable, and useful.
      </p>

      <p>
        In particular, examine the feature levels. State features, move features,
        segment features, attempt features, and cohort features answer different
        questions. A common failure mode in process-data work is to compute a
        feature at one level and interpret it at another.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-reader-guide-ch6">
      <title>Chapter 6: Use Features to Diagnose Items</title>

      <p>
        <xref ref="ch-visualization-diagnostics" text="title"/> shows what the
        feature framework buys in practice. Placement heatmaps show where each
        card tends to go. Distance and progress curves show movement toward or
        away from the key. Transition displays show common moves through the
        state space. Error-profile displays show the structure of wrong answers.
      </p>

      <p>
        The displays are descriptive. They are meant to reveal patterns worth
        explaining, not to prove the explanation. For example, a decoy slot that
        attracts many cards may suggest a design issue, a misconception, or a
        response strategy, but the display alone does not decide among these.
      </p>

      <p>
        While reading, ask which displays would be most compelling in a methods
        article. A small number of polished diagnostics on real data may be more
        persuasive than a long catalog of possible plots.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-reader-guide-ch7">
      <title>Chapter 7: Make the Pipeline Reproducible</title>

      <p>
        <xref ref="ch-data-processing" text="title"/> may be the most important
        chapter for a data scientist. It explains that a raw drag-and-drop export
        is not yet a trajectory. The pipeline must parse raw rows, order events,
        decode positional labels using item maps, replay moves, validate check
        anchors, flag questionable traces, define attempts and sessions, and
        export analysis-ready tables.
      </p>

      <p>
        The most important distinction is
        <md>
          \text{raw row}
          \neq
          \text{reconstructed state}
          \neq
          \text{derived feature}.
        </md>
        Raw rows should be retained. Reconstructed states should carry quality
        flags. Features should be computed only after the reconstruction status is
        known.
      </p>

      <p>
        Check rows are especially important because they contain a full submitted
        configuration. They can be used as anchors: the state reconstructed by
        replaying moves should agree with the state parsed from the check record.
        Disagreement does not always make the row useless, but it changes which
        features can be trusted.
      </p>

      <p>
        While reading, ask whether the pipeline is implementable by someone else.
        The best version of this chapter should specify enough table structure,
        flags, provenance, and inclusion rules to support a reproducible analysis
        or reference implementation.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-reader-guide-ch8">
      <title>Chapter 8: Ask Whether Exact Labels Are the Right Comparison</title>

      <p>
        <xref ref="ch-group-features" text="title"/> extends the
        baseline comparison. The platform grades exact labeled placement: each
        card must be in its authored home zone. But for some research questions,
        a wrong labeled answer may still have meaningful grouping structure.
      </p>

      <p>
        The chapter introduces label-free agreement, partition views, merge and
        split features, pairwise grouping metrics, and weighted or semantic
        distances. These are diagnostics, not replacements for the grader, unless
        the research question explicitly concerns grouping rather than labeled
        category choice.
      </p>

      <p>
        While reading, ask whether these advanced features belong in the main
        article. They may be important for the broader framework, but a first
        publication might present them as extensions after the baseline pipeline
        has been established.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-reader-guide-ch9">
      <title>Chapter 9: Model Patterns Without Forgetting the Data Structure</title>

      <p>
        <xref ref="ch-statistical-modeling" text="title"/> asks how the feature
        tables can support inference. The data are nested and crossed: events sit
        inside attempts; attempts involve students and items; students encounter
        many items; items are encountered by many students; and item versions or
        feedback policies may change across semesters.
      </p>

      <p>
        The modeling chapter therefore emphasizes student effects, item effects,
        design covariates, process features, temporal alignment of predictors and
        outcomes, and validation across students, items, or semesters.
      </p>

      <p>
        The key reading discipline is to separate prediction, explanation, and
        causal interpretation. A feature may improve prediction without explaining
        the mechanism. A coefficient may describe an association without showing
        that a behavior caused success. A process feature that occurs after the
        outcome should not be used to predict that outcome.
      </p>

      <p>
        While reading, ask what the primary modeling target should be. Possible
        targets include first-check correctness, eventual correctness, first-check
        distance, post-check gain, number of moves, active time, and item-level
        error rates. A publishable article should probably choose a small number
        of primary outcomes.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-reader-guide-ch10">
      <title>Chapter 10: Keep the Interpretations Honest</title>

      <p>
        <xref ref="ch-validity-interpretation" text="title"/> states the boundary
        of the framework. The logs directly record moves, checks, resets,
        feedback, timestamps, and reconstructed states. They do not directly
        record understanding, misconception, strategy, intent, attention,
        learning, or causality.
      </p>

      <p>
        The most important contribution of this chapter is the claim ladder:
        observation, structural feature, response pattern, interpretation, and
        validated claim. The framework strongly supports the first three levels.
        The fourth requires careful language. The fifth requires additional
        evidence such as related items, written explanations, interviews,
        pre/post comparisons, randomized feedback conditions, or cross-semester
        stability.
      </p>

      <p>
        This chapter should influence the whole article, not only the conclusion.
        If the paper uses restrained interpretive language from the beginning, the
        framework will be more credible.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-reader-guide-core-questions">
    <title>Brainstorming Questions</title>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-reader-guide-framework-questions">
      <title>Framework and Notation</title>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Is the membership-matrix notation useful enough to keep central?</p></li>
          <li><p>Would vector notation be preferred for implementation and matrix notation only for aggregation?</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-reader-guide-feature-questions">
      <title>Feature Engineering</title>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Which features are likely redundant, unstable, or too dependent on item design?</p></li>
          <li><p>Which features are robust to logging irregularities?</p></li>
          <li><p>Which advanced comparison features have enough empirical payoff to justify inclusion in the main article?</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-reader-guide-modeling-questions">
      <title>Statistical Modeling</title>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Is anything still missing in the analysis-ready table?</p></li>
          <li><p>Are the proposed models realistic for the expected sample size and item count?</p></li>
          <li><p>Do process features add predictive value beyond final correctness, item identity, and student identity?</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-reader-guide-validity-questions">
      <title>Validity and Interpretation</title>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Which claims can be supported from logs alone?</p></li>
          <li><p>How should feedback-policy changes be handled when comparing across platform eras?</p></li>
          <li><p>Could observed patterns reflect interface layout or item wording rather than mathematical understanding?</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-reader-guide-what-feedback-to-return">
      <title>In General ...</title>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Which chapters or sections should form the core article?</p></li>
          <li><p>Which definitions are necessary, unclear, or overly formal?</p></li>
          <li><p>Which feature families seem most valuable for real data analysis?</p></li>
          <li><p>What one empirical example would best demonstrate the framework?</p></li>
          <li><p>What should be cut or moved to an appendix?</p></li>
          <li><p>Where would the framework lose a skeptical data-science reader?</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

</chapter>
